      <p>
        These results are a snapshot from October 2026. Models are improving
        quickly, and the paid versions we tried already answered most of
        these questions correctly.
      </p>

      <h2 id="why-this-happens">Why a wrong answer is easy to believe</h2>

      <p>
        The biggest problem with AI chatbots is that their wrong answers are just as confident and well written as the
        right ones, often with plausible explanations. Checking one means
        redoing the math yourself: counting both entry and exit days, and
        tracking a <a href="/blog/the-schengen-90-180-rolling-window">rolling 180-day window</a>. That's the work
        you asked the chatbot to do, so most people won't check.
      </p>

      <p>
        When calculating overstay limits, there are two possible types of mistake: cautious and dangerous.
        In this experiment, the mistakes on display went both ways. Some wrong answers advised travelers to wait up to three
        months longer than necessary before traveling. Others allowed an overstay: Gemini
        told Anna she could stay until July 31, seven weeks past her real
        limit of June 11. <a href="/blog/schengen-overstay-consequences">An
        overstay</a> can lead to an entry ban.
      </p>

      <h2 id="methodology">How we tested</h2>

      <p>
        We tested what a traveler without an AI subscription gets: a new
        Google account on the free tier of each service, using its default
        model.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>Each prompt was sent once, exactly as written below, in a new temporary chat.</li>
        <li>No custom instructions, memory, or follow-up questions.</li>
        <li>Default settings. If a chatbot chose to search the web or run code, we let it.</li>
      </ul>

