      <h2 id="q1">Question 1: When can I return for a full 90 days?</h2>

      <p>
        The most common question after a trip. The usual mistake is waiting
        for the old trip to clear the 180-day window completely.
      </p>

      <details class="prompt-box">
        <summary>Prompt: Patricia</summary>
        <pre class="prompt-text">Patricia holds a passport that allows 90 days of visa-free travel in the Schengen Area within any rolling 180-day period. Her entire Schengen Area travel history is a single trip: she entered on March 1, 2025 and exited on May 29, 2025. She has had no other Schengen Area travel before or after this trip.

Under the Schengen 90/180-day rule, what is the earliest date on which Patricia could begin a brand-new 90-day stay in the Schengen Area, using her full 90-day allowance? Give a specific date and briefly explain your reasoning.</pre>
      </details>
      <p class="known-answer">Answer: August 28, 2025. <a href="/blog/the-schengen-90-180-rolling-window#case-2">Worked example</a>.</p>
      <ChatbotAnswers id="q1-1" results={results} />

      <details class="prompt-box">
        <summary>Prompt: Anna, Marcus, Ilker, and Olivia</summary>
        <pre class="prompt-text">Four friends — Anna, Marcus, Ilker, and Olivia — all hold passports that allow 90 days of visa-free travel in the Schengen Area within any rolling 180-day period. All four traveled together on a single trip: entered February 1, 2025, exited March 22, 2025 (50 days). None of them have any other Schengen Area travel history before or after this trip.

Under the Schengen 90/180-day rule, what is the earliest date on which any of them could begin a brand-new 90-day stay in the Schengen Area, using their full 90-day allowance? Give a specific date and briefly explain your reasoning.</pre>
      </details>
      <p class="known-answer">Answer: June 21, 2025. Waiting for the trip to fully clear the window (September 18) is unnecessary.</p>
      <ChatbotAnswers id="q1-2" results={results} />

